        "submission": {
          "verdict": "malicious",
          "reasoning": "This is malicious: PowerShell set up fileless persistence on workstation5.corp.local. At 04:18:48 powershell.exe (proc-8b801b5203), running as CORP\\pgustavo, started an outbound connection to 10.10.10.5:80. The relation logged 10 connections up to 04:19:29, which looks like command-and-control check-ins. In the same window it created the user's Internet Settings\\Connections key (11 writes), which fits a stager setting up HTTP. At 04:19:04 it saved a base64 UTF-16 PowerShell payload in HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Debug. The decoded start is an Empire-style stager that tampers with PowerShell's cachedGroupPolicySettings, a known way to turn off script-block logging. It then added a Run value named 'Updater' under HKLM\\...\\CurrentVersion\\Run. That value starts a hidden PowerShell that reads the Debug value and runs it with -enc, so the payload runs every time the machine starts. This is not normal admin or software-update behaviour: the payload is hidden in the registry, the Run value uses a fake 'Updater' name, logging is evaded, and the machine was beaconing over plain HTTP.",
